, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"id" : "obj-18",
					"linecount" : 28,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 716.0, 243.0, 431.0, 382.0 ],
					"text" : "Noticing that there were 2 outputs, I divided my gen patch into 2 main alogrithms like always.  \n\nOutput 1:\nI used week 8's lesson on frequent modulation, containing a phasor for the Hz, carrier, modulation, and index, all while combining them using the go.harmonic feature. By having all these necessary inputs, it nicely broke down the sound process into neat categories that I could individual change. I incorporated appropriate scaling for each input, as the initial ranges for the \"date-and-time\" did not numerically fit the Hz, carrier, and modulation parameters. After, I then floored each of the scaling outputs to ensure that only integers will be passed through. At the end, I added a go.onepole.basic.hz feature to filter out all the high-pitched noises that were unpleasant to the ear, acting as a low-pass filter. For the index, I used the raw output of the \"seconds\", passed it through a sin function and then multiplied it by 1.5. This was in hopes of creating a frequently increasing index value for intrigue, as well as smoothening the initial data. Finally, with the \"days\" output, I used a slide and scale function to create a modulating sound, going in and out, also to have more interesting audio. \n\nOutput 2:\nThis alogrithm was based off of chp5 \"mixed-sequencer\". I really enoyed the short bursts of notes, through the MIDI function, as well as the element of sporadic randomness. I modified it so that the go.ramp.div features intook the \"hours\" parameter as their new ratio input. I scaled the source phasor's input to a new range, one that is capable of effective audio variation. Gates are included afterwards, with specific timed parameters, allowing the signal to continue after the first quarter, third, and half. The output then gets converted from MIDI to frequency (Hz) into a cycle that is then amplified by 1.5x. "
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"fontname" : "Arial Bold",
					"fontsize" : 18.0,
					"id" : "obj-19",
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 716.0, 208.0, 192.0, 27.0 ],
					"text" : "My process:"
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"fontname" : "Arial Bold",
					"fontsize" : 18.0,
					"id" : "obj-31",
					"linecount" : 2,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 1152.0, 206.0, 192.0, 47.0 ],
					"text" : "Brief inspiration and description:"
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
					"id" : "obj-29",
					"linecount" : 14,
					"maxclass" : "comment",
					"numinlets" : 1,
					"numoutlets" : 0,
					"patching_rect" : [ 1152.0, 268.0, 396.0, 194.0 ],
					"text" : "After reading the assignment's description, I completely understand the need for an upbeat radio station, different from the melancholy tunes airing now. It said that the station we create should be enjoyed on walks, doing chores, and other activities. I would not go as far as to say that I would listen to this assignment for hours upon hours; however, if I were to match my preferences to a robotic counterpart, then I would say that this station fulfills that. I hoped to instil a sense of mechanical whimsy in the listener. This wish is what inspired me to lean into soft tapping tones and a calming, sustained undertone. No matter the time and day you listen to this radio station, the overall jubilance is evident (of course, following the requirements, everytime it is different - frequency, tempo, modulation, etc). And as the cherry on top, to further hone in on this happy robot radio station vibe, I titled it \"Domo Arigato Mr. Roboto\", a playful combination of words, as well as a beloved song. "
				}

			}
, 			{
				"box" : 				{
